    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-018-0272-2",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Palaeoclimatology",
    "problem": "The main obstacle in quantifying Eocene climate evolution is the lack of continuous, high-quality tropical temperature reconstructions, as existing records are too fragmentary and low-resolution to test competing hypotheses about the drivers of climate change (greenhouse gases versus ocean circulation).",
    "method": "This paper presents a new continuous Eocene equatorial sea surface temperature record from biomarkers, which is combined with existing data into a multi-proxy stack to show that greenhouse gas forcing, rather than ocean circulation, was the main driver of Eocene climate.",
    "task": "The task is to reconstruct a continuous Eocene equatorial sea surface temperature record to investigate the relationship between tropical and polar climate evolution and determine the main drivers of Eocene climate, such as greenhouse gas forcing versus ocean circulation changes.",
