    "innovation": "This paper presents a continuous Eocene equatorial sea surface temperature record, demonstrating that tropical and deep-ocean temperatures changed in parallel, which supports greenhouse gas forcing, rather than ocean circulation changes, as the main driver of Eocene climate.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "fully coupled",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Our temperature proxy compilations provide a concrete and robust test of the ability of models to reproduce past warm climates under increased greenhouse gas forcing. We performed fully coupled general circulation model simulations using the NCAR Community Earth System Model, version 1 (CESM 1), by applying a range of radiative forcings equivalent to a range of Eocene CO2 concentrations\u2014560 parts per million (p.p.m.), 1,120 p.p.m., 2,240 p.p.m. and 4,480 p.p.m.; simulations EO1\u2013EO4, respectively (see Methods)\u2014run to full equilibrium.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "equilibrium",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "The CESM 1 simulations share the same generalized Eocene palaeogeography to assess the effect of changing CO2 by itself, and were all run for more than 3,000 yr to equilibrium. Simulations using an earlier, and generally similar, version of this model were found to produce the best match to early Eocene proxy temperatures within a multi-model ensemble15, and preliminary comparison revealed that these new simulations are slightly improved over the earlier version for the early Eocene20. Results from the lower-CO2 simulations (560 p.p.m. and 1,120 p.p.m.) and further information on the model can be found in Goldner et al. 26. This version of CESM has a modern \u2018fast\u2019 climate sensitivity of 2.9 \u00b0C per CO2 doubling and a nearly constant \u2018slow\u2019 climate sensitivity (ESS) of 3.5 \u00b0C per CO2 doubling in the Eocene simulations used here.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "Earth system sensitivity",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "However, our model requires much larger changes in CO2 to produce the large and dynamic range of Eocene tropical SST and deep-sea temperature than that reconstructed from proxy data. This implies that the Earth system sensitivity\u00b9\u00b9 to CO2 doubling derived from the model (3.5 \u00b0C) is too low to create sufficient warmth. We consider available Eocene CO2 reconstructions in combination with our proxy- and model-based temperatures",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "CESM 1",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
